12":[["常陸太田市",0,0],["水府村",0,2004],["里美村",0,2004],["金砂郷町",0,2004]],"08216":[["笠間市",0,0],["友部町",0,2006],["岩間町",0,2006]],"08217":[["取手市",0,0],["藤代町",0,2005]],"08220":[["つくば市",0,0],["茎崎町",0,2002]],"08223":[["潮来町",0,2001],["牛堀町",0,2001]],"08224":[["守谷町",0,2002]],"08225":[["大宮町",0,2004],["山方町",0,2004],["御前山村",0,2004],["緒川村",0,2004],["美和村",0,2004]],"08226":[["瓜連町",0,2005],["那珂町",0,2005]],"08227":[["下館市",0,2005],["協和町",0,2005],["明野町",0,2005],["関城町",0,2005]],"08228":[["岩井市",0,2005],["猿島町",0,2005]],"08229":[["新利根村",0,1996],["東村",0,1996],["桜川村",0,2005],["江戸崎町",0,2005]],"08230":[["出島村",0,1997],["千代田町",0,2005]],"08231":[["大和村",0,2005],["岩瀬町",0,2005],["真壁町",0,2005]],"08232":[["波崎町",0,2005],["神栖町",0,2005]],"08233":[["北浦村",0,1997],["玉造町",0,2005],["麻生町",0,2005]],"08234":[["大洋村",0,2005],["旭村",0,2005],["鉾田町",0,2005]],"08235":[["伊奈町",0,2006],["谷和原村",0,2006]],"08236":[["小川町",0,2006],["玉里村",0,2006],["美野里町",0,2006]],"08310":[["七会村",0,2005],["常北町",0,2005],["桂村",0,2005]],"08447":[["河内村",0,1996]],"08542":[["五霞村",0,1996]]},"yurai":{"8":{"水戸市":"Mito, like minato, was an old word for the mouth of a river or the sea: the tip of the plateau between the Naka River and Lake Senba was called mito. The spelling 水戸 first appears in Yoshida Yakuōin documents of about 1400.","つくば市":"Named after Mount Tsukuba. Governor Takeuchi Fujio pushed for hiragana because 筑 is easily misread \"chiku\", and the kana looked simpler and more modern — the first hiragana city name in Ibaraki.","日立市":"Hitachi first appears as an administrative name in 1889, when Miyata and Namekawa villages merged as Hitachi Village. A legend credits Tokugawa Mitsukuni, but the city's historians stress Satō Takatada. In 1939 the fame of the Hitachi company helped settle the city name.","ひたちなか市":"At the centre of old Hitachi Province and once part of Naka District. Katsuta and Nakaminato also considered \"Hitachinaka\" in kanji or \"Katsutaminato\", but chose Ibaraki's second hiragana city name.","土浦市":"Theories vary: borrowed from Tsuchiura Village (now part of Miho), the \"Tsuchi\" of the Tsuchiya domain plus \"ura\" (shore), or a corruption of tsutsu-uraura, \"every port and shore\".","筑西市":"\"West of Mount Tsukuba.\" Finalists included \"Kita-Tsukuba\", and a strong contender was \"Shiawase\" (happiness), spelled from the first syllables of the four merging towns.","牛久市":"Probably from an old word, though no one is sure. A tale from Kinryū-ji — a lazy acolyte turned into a cow and drowned in the marsh, the \"cow-eating marsh\" — circulates almost as fact. \"Ushioku\" (tidal inlet) is another proposal.","龍ケ崎市":"Usually traced to the land steward Shimokōbe Masayoshi, who lost his lands as an in-law of Minamoto no Yoshitsune and took the name Ryūzaki. Other tales involve frequent tornadoes or a dragon falling from the sky.","守谷市":"Three unproven stories: Yamato Takeru marvelling at the forest (\"mori naru kana\"); Taira no Masakado calling it \"a valley easy to defend\" (though the castle was built later by the Sōma); or guards (mori) of a district office.","坂東市":"\"Bandō\" was the old name for the whole Kantō — the lands east of the \"slopes\" of the Ashigara and Usui passes. Chosen in 2003 for a city near the region's centre, it also echoes \"Bandō Tarō\", the Tone River's nickname.","つくばみらい市":"The whole area was Tsukuba District, and \"Mirai-daira\" is its Tsukuba Express station. A city name containing \"future\" (mirai) was unprecedented — and at six kana it is among Japan's longest."}},"kyu":{"08201":{"t":76,"k":48324,"r":[["Mito Domain",36784],["@hatamoto",4639],["Shishido Domain",3742],["@jisha",1358],["@other",1801]]},"08202":{"t":36,"k":25878,"r":[["Mito Domain",25419],["@jisha",439],["@hatamoto",20]]},"08203":{"t":49,"k":28378,"r":[["Tsuchiura Domain",27538],["@bakufu",471],["@hatamoto",353],["@jisha",17]]},"08204":{"t":58,"k":27219,"r":[["@bakufu",11156],["Koga Domain",9411],["Mibu Domain",2425],["Sekiyado Domain",2051],["@other",2176]]},"08205":{"t":64,"k":45002,"r":[["@hatamoto",21771